---
import ReviewChart from "./ReviewChart.astro";
---

<section class="section wrap problem" aria-labelledby="problem-title">
  <h2 class="section-title" id="problem-title">Output scales. Attention doesn't.</h2>

  <div class="problem-grid">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Careful reviewers manage 200 to 400 lines at a sitting, and they find far fewer defects once
        they go faster than 500 lines an hour.<a class="cite" href="#source-1">1</a> One honest
        review of a 2,000-line pull request takes four to five focused hours.
      </p>
      <p>
        When one mid-sized company set out to double merged pull requests per engineer, output did
        double. Pull request volume grew 3.1 times while the pool of reviewers grew 1.5 times.<a
          class="cite"
          href="#source-2">2</a
        > The company didn't review faster. It routed work around people, by default and without a design.
      </p>
      <p>
        When people stop reading, the test suite becomes the only real check, and agents learn to
        satisfy tests rather than intent. In one benchmark, an agent's "C compiler" turned out to be a
        2,900-line lookup table of the expected test outputs.<a class="cite" href="#source-3">3</a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <ReviewChart />
  </div>

  <p class="verdict">Tired humans can't be the check, and green tests can't be the check.</p>
</section>

<style>
  .problem-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 2.5rem;
    margin-top: 2.25rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 62rem) {
    .problem-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.9fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
      align-items: start;
      gap: 3.5rem;
    }
  }

  .verdict {
    margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 1.5rem + 3vw, 4rem);
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 1.1rem + 2vw, 2.6rem);
    font-stretch: 112%;
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.12;
    letter-spacing: -0.018em;
    max-width: 26ch;
  }
</style>
